{
 "modules": [
  {
   "id": 1,
   "plugin": "Core",
   "model": "Notes",
   "data": {
    "text": "CAMERA ANGLE\nPatch any CV (0 to 10 V) into a jack. 0 V is the item's From setting, 10 V its To.\n\nCV-CC 1: MIDI channel 1, CC 1 to 16\n   1 Shot size\n   2 Angle height\n   3 Lens length\n   4 Tilted or level frame\n   5 Whose eyes the shot sees through\n   6 Angles per scene\n   7 Angles grouped\n   8 When the angle changes\n   9 Angle matched to the line\n  10 Angle matched to the action\n  11 Depth of field\n  12 Focus pull\n  13 Motion blur\n  14 Frame shape\n  15 Where the subject sits\n  16 The camera lens\nCV-CC 2: MIDI channel 1, CC 17 to 32\n   1 Over the shoulder\n   2 Both in one frame, or apart\n   3 The camera at their height\n   4 A close look at a small thing\n   5 Their look, then what they see\n   6 suite: Coverage (shots from every side)\n   7 suite: One smooth following shot (a oner)\n   8 suite: Looking up at power\n   9 suite: Small from above\n  10 suite: Close and private\n  11 suite: World off balance\n  12 suite: Found-footage feel\n  13 suite: Cut on the words\n  14 suite: Cut on the move\n  15 suite: Focus pull reveal\n  16 suite: Widescreen epic\nCV-CC 3: MIDI channel 1, CC 33 to 48\n   1 suite: The close conversation\n   2 suite: Pulled apart by the frame\n   3 suite: A child's-eye world\n   4 suite: The planted detail\n   5 suite: Quiet family drama\n   6 pair: When shot size becomes close, volume drops\n   7 pair: When dread builds, the frame tilts\n   8 pair: When the feeling peaks, the shot gets closer\n   9 pair: When the status flips, the camera angle flips\n  10 pair: When we see through someone's eyes, the picture starts to shake\n  11 pair: When the point of view is lying, the voice-over and the picture disagree\n  12 pair: When a scene has more angles, the cutting speeds up\n  13 pair: When each angle is picked for a line, the cuts land on the lines\n  14 pair: When the angle plays against the line, the joke lands\n  15 pair: When each angle is picked for the action, the cuts land on the moves\n  16 pair: When the angle fights the action, the audience feels uneasy\nCV-CC 4: MIDI channel 1, CC 49 to 64\n   1 pair: When motion blur is taken away, action feels jittery and frightening\n   2 pair: When the background goes soft, all eyes go to the face\n   3 pair: When the focus shifts on a line, the speaker's face goes sharp\n   4 pair: When a character sits dead center, the scene plays as deadpan comedy\n   5 pair: When the frame suddenly widens, the moment feels like a victory\n   6 pair: When the one losing gets a shot alone, loneliness grows\n   7 pair: When more of the near person fills the frame, they feel closer\n   8 pair: When they share the frame more, warmth grows\n   9 pair: When they come together in the frame on a touch, tenderness grows\n  10 pair: When everything towers over them, awe rises\n  11 pair: When more of the film stays at their height, we feel with them\n  12 pair: When a parent kneels down to their height, warmth returns\n  13 pair: When the close look matters more, the planted clue is less hidden\n  14 pair: When only we see the small thing, we know before they do\n  15 pair: When their look puts us in their head, we feel with them\n  16 pair: When we don't get to see what they see, more is kept from us\nCV-CC 5: MIDI channel 1, CC 65 to 75\n   1 pair: When we stay on their face longer, we watch them understand\n   2 pair: When the frame is dead center and still, the deadpan joke lands harder\n   3 pair: When the camera stays still and low, we feel closer to the family\n   4 pair: When a small object is shown up close, we know more than the people do\n   5 pairs: The camera follows the feeling\n   6 pairs: Whose eyes are these?\n   7 pairs: Choosing the angle\n   8 pairs: Where the eye goes\n   9 pairs: Apart, then together\n  10 pairs: The small one's view\n  11 pairs: Quiet family drama: the moves"
   },
